This page has no mathematical derivation. The main text, upright in ink, is a private family letter headed 'Quickborn den 3 Aug. 1853', addressed 'Lieber Wilhelm!'. Wilhelm is very probably Riemann's brother; Riemann's father was pastor at Quickborn. The letter describes preparations for the church and school visitation (Kirchen- und Schulvisitation) on the coming Sunday:
- guests arrive at 8;
- the service begins at 9½, followed by the catechisation (Katechisation) and the visitation business held in the parsonage;
- at 1 o'clock the schoolteachers (apparently Schreib and Ahlers) catechise with their pupils;
- on Monday at 1 the Superintendent intends to visit two village schools nearby, and the father is to attend if his health allows;
- the programme had been sent about a fortnight earlier, the 'Kirchenzettel' (list of hymns/service order) is fixed, and provisions for entertaining the guests have been brought in;
- the father considered inviting further acquaintances to the meal but found none suitable.

The bottom of the sheet, written upside down in pencil, holds a faint, largely struck-through draft text, which is illegible, and two numbers: 9.55 and 9.58800. These numbers match the logarithm-style figures on the next page, where '9,55800' appears in a computation tied to 'Traité de Mécanique No. 323, pag. 638', probably Poisson's treatise. They are most plausibly common logarithms in the usual -10 convention: 9.558 corresponds to log10 of about 0.3614. That is my interpretation; it cannot be confirmed from this page alone. No problem is set up or solved here, and the sheet was evidently reused as scrap paper for numerical work.
